<p>Joe Lemire of Sports Business Journal has a <a href="https://www.sportsbusinessjournal.com/Articles/2026/08/31/a-fan-built-an-alternate-mlb-app-the-league-hired-him/">detailed story</a> about how MLB came to hire Jason Weingradt, the developer behind the <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/08/mlb-hires-indie-mosaic-app-developer/">amazing baseball app Mosaic</a>:</p>
<blockquote><p>
  By drawing on public application programming interfaces (APIs) from the league and other sources, Weingradt built an app in TestFlight — Apple’s home for products still in beta — and shared his new creation, Mosaic, by posting on Threads and tagging #buildinpublic to solicit early adopter feedback….</p>
<p>  That very same day, MLB Executive Vice President Kenny Gersh received a Slack message from colleagues alerting him to Mosaic and asking if the league’s legal team should get involved. While of course not wanting just any developer to profit from baseball’s IP, Gersh appreciated that this was a controlled environment that required an MLB.tv subscription to access premium features, so he replied, “Not ideal, but it’s not a huge problem yet. Maybe we’ll hire.”
</p></blockquote>
<p>They hired. I know there’s a lot of skepticism out there about whether the big organization will just crush the Little App That Could. To me, it says a lot about MLB’s tech brain trust that they embraced Weingradt and that he’s still dropping incredibly impressive new features into the app on a regular basis.</p>
